# The colon fence's sigil family

Three colon fences take a punctuation mark where the others take a word. The
sigil is the only character on the opener that says which container it is.

The line block keeps its line structure.

::: |
Roses are red,
  violets are blue,
this line keeps its indent too.
:::

The fenced block quote builds the quote the marker-prefixed form builds,
without a marker on every line.

::: >
A quote written as a fence.

It can hold more than one paragraph, and needs no marker on any line.
:::

The local hard-break block turns every soft break in its direct paragraph
children into a hard break.

::: \
Every line here
ends with a hard break,
including this one.
:::

Each nests inside the others, with a longer fence.

:::: >
A quote holding a line block.

::: |
one
two
:::
::::
